 "text": "The mission of Chicago’s Department of Housing is to expand access and choice for residents and protect their rights to quality homes that are affordable, safe, and healthy.\nResource Finder Tool\nIn English\nEn Español\nFeatured Services and Programs\nFor Renters\nResources and information for Chicago renters and leaseholders.\nFor Landlords\nResources and information for Chicago landlords and property managers.\nFor Homebuyers\nResources for people seeking to purchase a home in Chicago.\nFor Homeowners\nResources for Chicago residents who own a home.\nFor Developers\nResources and programs for developers of affordable housing in Chicago.\nFor Lenders\nInformation for lenders and financial organizations.\nResources for 2-4 Unit Owners\nDOH maintains a list of resources for use by smaller landlords who own two to four residential units.\nCommunity Outreach and Engagement\nLearn about and request DOH for community engagement and outreach events.\nData, Plans and Reports\nDOH data, plans and quarterly progress reports.\nAffordable Requirements Ordinance\nThe ARO requires large residential developments to provide a percentage of units at affordable prices.\nQAP\nThe 2025 Qualified Allocation Plan (QAP) will help DOH direct federal affordable housing funds to the neighborhoods where they are most needed.\nATSM\nThe ATSM contains the policies and guidelines for construction of multifamily affordable housing development projects in Chicago.\nMost Recent News\nOct 14, 2026\nDepartment of Housing Proposes Additional Funding for Affordable Senior Housing Development in Humboldt Park\nOct 14, 2026\nDepartment of Housing Proposes Financing for 70 Affordable Rental Units at Morgan Park Commons\nOct 14, 2026\nDepartment of Housing Proposes Funding to Preserve Fully Affordable Housing Near Palmer Square Park\nOct 14, 2026\nDepartment of Housing Seeks Council Approval for Additional Funding for Oak and Larrabee Mixed-Income Development\nOct 14, 2026\nDepartment of Housin